WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.660
An outbound HTTP client may look like ordinary plumbing, but in a C++ application it is often a security boundary.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:16.086
The client decides where data is sent, how the destination is authenticated, which credentials are attached, how redirects are handled, how responses

3
00:00:16.086 --> 00:00:21.448
are parsed, and what happens when the remote service is slow, unavailable, or unexpected.

4
00:00:21.448 --> 00:00:26.427
That boundary matters because outbound requests connect local application state to external systems.

5
00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:32.938
A request may carry sensitive records, service tokens, cookies, client certificates, tenant identifiers, or internal workflow data.

6
00:00:32.938 --> 00:00:39.066
A response may influence business state, update caches, trigger processing, or become input to a parser.

7
00:00:39.066 --> 00:00:46.726
Even when the remote service is owned by the same organization, the client still crosses a trust and availability boundary.

8
00:00:46.726 --> 00:00:50.173
Secure HTTP client design makes the important choices explicit.

9
00:00:50.173 --> 00:00:52.471
The intended destination should be clear.

10
00:00:52.471 --> 00:00:55.535
TLS and endpoint identity rules should be documented.

11
00:00:55.535 --> 00:00:58.599
Credentials should come from approved secret handling mechanisms.

12
00:00:58.599 --> 00:01:02.429
Request construction should preserve structure instead of splicing text casually.

13
00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.642
Response handling should use validation, size limits, and predictable error behavior.

14
00:01:06.642 --> 00:01:08.557
The defensive goal is reviewability.

15
00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:18.132
A maintainer should be able to inspect an HTTP client and understand what service it contacts, what data it sends, what trust policy it applies,

16
00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:21.962
which failures stop the operation, and what diagnostics are recorded.

17
00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:31.154
Treating the client as a boundary keeps those decisions visible and turns network behavior into something the team can test, monitor, and govern over

18
00:01:31.154 --> 00:01:31.920
time safely.
